Title: EP01 — What's a Spec?
Credit: Written by
Author: vibecode.town
Series: Vibecode Town Season 1
Source: Act 1 — "What's a Spec?"
Draft date: 2026-03-17
Contact: vibecode.town
Duration: ~210 seconds (3:30)
Notes: 2D flat vector style (Kurzgesagt level). Narrator V.O. only. Vee never speaks. Max 6 Vee reactions.

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 1: HOOK [0:00–0:15]                                      */
/* emotion_curve: EXCITEMENT                                        */
/* narrative_stage: HOOK                                             */
/* open_loop: "Why did everything break?"                           */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */

INT. VEE'S DESK - NIGHT

A single monitor glows in a dim room. Code scrolls. Dashboards pulse. A terminal blinks green. Everything is running.

/* vee_reaction: 1/6 */
/* shorts_candidate */

Vee throws both arms up. Huge grin. Eyes wide behind her round black glasses. The yellow hoodie sleeves flop past her wrists. "BUILD SUCCESS" flashes green on screen.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This is the moment every vibe coder lives for. You told AI what to build. And it built it.

= BEAT. One second of silence.

The monitor flickers. The green text turns amber. Then red. Error messages cascade down the screen — line after line after line.

Vee's grin vanishes. Her arms lower slowly. She stares at a wall of text she cannot read.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And then it all broke. Why?

> TITLE CARD: **WHAT'S A SPEC?**

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 2: PROBLEM [0:15–0:45]                                   */
/* emotion_curve: EXCITEMENT → CONFIDENCE → first crack of FEAR     */
/* narrative_stage: PROBLEM                                         */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: motion_graphic | space: desk */

INT. VEE'S DESK - DAY (THREE DAYS EARLIER)

> INSERT: "Three days earlier."

Bright room. Sunlight. Vee sits at a clean desk. One empty terminal window.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Three days ago, Vee discovered AI coding. And it felt like magic.

= Vee types a single line. A complete app scaffolds itself on screen — folders, files, components, a running preview. Instant.

/* vee_reaction: 2/6 */

Vee's eyes go wide. She leans forward, mouth slightly open. Pure wonder.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Type what you want. AI builds it. A to-do app? Done. A database? Done. A login page with auth? Done.

= MONTAGE — quick cuts, each 2 seconds: Vee types → app appears. Types → app appears. Types → app appears. Her grin gets wider each time. The screen fills with more and more windows.

/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */

NARRATOR (V.O.)
By day three she had a thousand lines of code across a dozen files. She couldn't explain what any of it did. But it ran. So she assumed it worked.

= Vee leans back, arms crossed, nodding. Confident.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She never asked herself one question. The only question that matters.

= BEAT. Half a second.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
What am I building?

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 3: CORE [0:45–3:00]                                     */
/* emotion_curve: FEAR → CLARITY                                    */
/* narrative_stage: CORE                                            */
/* duration: ~135 seconds                                          */
/* This is the EXTENDED METAPHOR segment: Building = Code           */
/* ================================================================ */

/* extended_metaphor_start */

/* visual_type: motion_graphic | space: whiteboard */

= TRANSITION: The desk fades. Background brightens to clean white. A flat, empty lot appears — simple 2D ground line, pale sky.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Let's talk about buildings. Real ones.

= A glowing blue rectangle unfolds on the left side of the frame — a blueprint. Clean lines. Labeled sections: FOUNDATION, FRAME, WALLS, ROOF.

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When you build a house, you start with a blueprint. It tells you the order.

= The blueprint animates step by step. On the right, a building rises in sync:

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Foundation first.

= A grey slab slides into place on the lot. Solid. Level.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Then the frame.

= Vertical beams rise from the foundation. Horizontal beams connect them. A clean skeleton.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Walls.

= Flat colored panels fill in between the beams. Orange and warm cream. The structure takes shape.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Roof.

= A simple angled roof lowers onto the top. A green checkmark pops above the finished house. Tiny sparkle particles.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Done. It stands because every piece knows where it goes. The blueprint told it.

= BEAT. The finished house sits solid on the right. The blueprint glows faintly on the left.

/* shorts_candidate */

= The blueprint fades out. The house slides off-frame. The empty lot reappears.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Now. Take away the blueprint.

= A red "X" stamps where the blueprint was. The lot is bare again.

/* visual_type: motion_graphic | space: whiteboard */

NARRATOR (V.O.)
No plan. No order. Just... start stacking.

= Colored blocks begin dropping from above — random sizes, random colors. They land at odd angles. Some balance. Some don't.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A wall before there's a foundation.

= A tall orange panel lands on bare ground. It wobbles.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A roof before there are walls.

= A roof triangle drops on top of nothing. It sits crooked.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Three different staircases going to the same floor.

= Three staircases appear side by side, crammed together, each a different style and color. Absurd.

= The tower of mismatched blocks grows taller. It leans. The camera follows it up. It sways.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It looks like a building. Kinda. If you squint.

= The structure sways harder. A red block at the base cracks.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And then —

= The tower COLLAPSES. Blocks tumble outward. Dust cloud. One block bounces toward the camera.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Everything falls down.

= Silence. 1.5 seconds. Rubble on the ground.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That's what happened to Vee's code. She stacked blocks without a blueprint. And the tower fell.

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */

= The rubble fades. Clean white space.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So what's the blueprint for code?

= A single word appears in bold, center frame: **SPEC**

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A spec.

= The word pulses once with a faint blue glow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And before you close this tab — a spec is not a hundred-page document. It's not a Jira board. It's not a meeting that should've been an email.

= Each rejected item appears and gets crossed out with a red X: a thick binder, a kanban board, a conference table.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A spec is one sentence.

= The crossed-out items sweep off screen. One clean line of text writes itself:

= "What am I building, and why?"

NARRATOR (V.O.)
"What am I building, and why?" That's it. One sentence. Everything else follows from that.

/* visual_type: motion_graphic | space: whiteboard */

= BEAT. The sentence stays on screen for 2 seconds.

= Below the sentence, a simple evolution diagram animates:

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Think of it like this.

= A skateboard appears. Four wheels. A plank. Minimal.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You don't start with a car. You start with a skateboard.

= An arrow points right. The skateboard morphs into a bicycle. Two wheels, pedals, handlebars.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Then a bicycle.

= Another arrow. The bicycle morphs into a small car. Simple, boxy, but complete.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Then a car. Each version works. Each version moves. But each one is a complete thing — not a random pile of car parts.

= Below the evolution line, a contrasting image: a heap of disconnected parts — a steering wheel, two doors, an engine, a windshield — sitting in a pile.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Without a spec, you get this. Parts. Not a vehicle. Parts that don't connect, built by an AI that doesn't know what the finished thing looks like. Because you never told it.

= The pile of parts fades.

/* extended_metaphor_end */

/* vee_reaction: 3/6 */
/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */

= CUT TO: Quick flash of Vee at her desk. She's staring at her monitor, head slightly tilted. A flicker of recognition crosses her face. 1.5 seconds.

= CUT BACK to whiteboard.

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */

= The "What am I building, and why?" sentence reappears. Below it, three bullet points write themselves:

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The spec gives you three things. One: a filter. When AI suggests something, you can ask — "Does this help me build my skateboard?" If no, skip it.

= First bullet: a funnel icon. Random blocks go in, only matching blocks come out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Two: a sequence. Foundation, then frame, then walls, then roof. You know the order.

= Second bullet: four numbered steps, connected by arrows.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Three: a definition of done. You know when to stop. Because you know what "finished" looks like.

= Third bullet: a simple finish flag icon.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Without those three things, you're stacking random blocks and hoping they hold.

= The three bullets compress into a small card labeled "SPEC" that glows blue.

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 4: APPLICATION [3:00–3:30]                               */
/* emotion_curve: CLARITY → GROWTH                                  */
/* narrative_stage: APPLICATION                                     */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */

INT. VEE'S DESK - NIGHT (PRESENT)

= Back to the opening scene. Same room. Same red errors on the monitor. Same dark glow.

But Vee isn't panicking.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Back to the present.

= Vee pushes her keyboard aside. She pulls out a piece of paper and a pencil.

/* vee_reaction: 4/6 */

= She turns away from the monitor. Faces the paper. Starts writing. Not code. Words.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She stopped coding. And she started thinking.

/* visual_type: diagram | space: desk */

= CU on the paper. Messy handwriting scratches across the surface:

= "I want to access my home files from any device."

= Below it: "Files sync. Connection stays alive. Works on phone."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
One sentence. That's all it took.

/* vee_reaction: 5/6 */

= Vee turns back to the monitor, paper in hand. The same red errors. But now she scans them differently. Her eyes track one specific line.

= One error highlights: "sync_agent: connection refused — no target host defined."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Same errors. Same wall of red. But now, one line jumps out. Because now she knows what she's looking for.

= Vee's expression shifts — a slow blink, a small nod. Not celebration. Understanding.

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 5: OUTRO [3:30–3:50]                                     */
/* emotion_curve: GROWTH → open loop for EP02                       */
/* narrative_stage: OUTRO                                           */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */

INT. VEE'S DESK - NIGHT (CONTINUOUS)

= Same composition as the HOOK. Monitor. Errors. Dark room.

Vee pins the piece of paper next to her monitor. It reads: "I want to access my home files from any device."

She picks up her mug. Takes a sip. Sets it down.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Everything is still broken.

= BEAT. One second.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But now she knows what to fix.

/* vee_reaction: 6/6 */

= Vee cracks her knuckles. Leans forward toward the keyboard. A tiny, determined half-smile.

= The camera slowly pulls back. The paper glows faintly beside the monitor — a small blue beacon in a room full of red.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
...at least, she thinks she does. Next time — she writes fifteen pages of specs. And somehow, that makes everything worse.

= BEAT.

> TITLE CARD: **NEXT: EP02 — "Twenty-Eight Left Arms"**

= FADE TO BLACK.

> END
